/**
 * Page templates — MTC-FCS-016, and the Mockup Maker imports.
 * ============================================================================
 * Four arrangements of the same website. Not four websites: the content model
 * does not change, the sections do not change, and nothing a contractor has
 * typed is tied to one of these. Only the shape of the page is.
 *
 * ── THEY ARE CSS, NOT FOUR COMPONENTS ──────────────────────────────────────
 * Every template renders the SAME markup, and `data-template` on the root is
 * what selects between them. That is a deliberate constraint, and it buys
 * three things worth more than the freedom it gives up:
 *
 *   1. Switching template can never lose content, because there is nothing to
 *      re-map — the DOM is identical.
 *   2. The preview, the downloaded file and the published page cannot disagree,
 *      because there is one renderer.
 *   3. A Freemium payment can be enforced server-side by rewriting ONE
 *      attribute. Four component trees would mean parsing the page to undo a
 *      template, which is not a thing to be doing on the publish path.
 *
 * ── SPLIT AND BOLD WERE REMOVED ────────────────────────────────────────────
 * They were arrangements of this builder's own markup. The five Mockup Maker
 * designs below are real pages with their own markup and stylesheets, so the
 * two kinds sat oddly beside each other in one list — and a contractor
 * choosing between nine things chooses worse than between seven.
 *
 * ── CLASSIC IS THE FREE ONE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Everything else is Premium, and `activeTemplate` in lib/site-draft.js is
 * what enforces that in the preview. The publish route enforces it for real.
 *
 * ── THE FIVE mm-* ARE THE MOCKUP MAKER'S DESIGNS ───────────────────────────
 * Imported from source--mockup-maker/src/templates. Their ids keep the maker's
 * own names (v2, v3, clean, studio, signature) behind an `mm-` prefix, because
 * those ids are what tie a design back to the repo it came from — v2 is
 * template--free-website-v2, and renaming it would throw that link away.
 *
 * They arrive as CSS rather than as the maker's React components on purpose.
 * That codebase renders each design from its own view model with its own theme
 * layer; porting five component trees would give this builder five ways to
 * lose content when somebody switches template, and would make the publish
 * route parse a page to undo a premium design instead of rewriting one
 * attribute. The LOOK is what was asked for, and the look is the CSS.
 */

/**
 * `worth` — what a designer would charge to build this page, in cents.
 * ============================================================================
 * MTC-FCS-052. It is the number the going-live toast slashes out, and it is a
 * COMPARISON, not a price of ours: nothing here has ever been sold for it and
 * nothing ever will be. It sits on the template because a heavier design is a
 * bigger job — which is what makes the figure move when somebody flips
 * through the designs.
 *
 * One number per design, declared here, so there is nowhere else for a price
 * to be typed and drift.
 */
